Percolation–Consensus Duality in Multi-Agent Discovery Networks

A Unified Threshold Theory (v0.3)

Ouadi Maakoul

AI: Claude, Deepseek, Meta 

Abstract

We develop a unified mathematical framework that connects three previously separate phenomena: percolation on time‑varying random graphs, DeGroot/Bayesian consensus dynamics, and combinatorial birth–death processes for idea generation. The model describes a heterogeneous network of human and AI agents jointly exploring a hypothesis space. We prove the existence of a sharp lower threshold for the emergence of a giant exploring component via temporal percolation, and we identify an upper threshold beyond which consensus forms too rapidly, causing premature false lock‑in (the Zollman regime). The central control parameter is the effective connectivity \rho = p / \gamma, where p is the edge probability in an Erdős–Rényi communication graph and \gamma is the spectral gap of the expected influence matrix, which governs the speed of belief homogenisation. We show that the lower threshold is \rho_c^- = 1/n, marking the onset of a percolating exploration cluster that enables explosive idea generation. The upper threshold is conjectured to scale as \rho_c^+ \sim C \log n / n, where the constant depends on the idea‑generation parameters. At this upper transition the rate of belief‑entropy collapse balances the combinatorial growth rate of the idea pool—a duality that unifies the order parameters of the two transitions. The proofs combine mean‑field percolation, spectral gap analysis, and a refined explosion criterion for birth–death processes with linear death, while the large‑deviation argument for the Zollman regime is recast as a first‑passage problem under a quasi‑decoupling assumption.

---

1. Introduction

When human researchers and large language models interact in a scientific network, three forces compete. Communication spreads ideas (percolation), social influence pulls beliefs toward a consensus, and combinatorial recombination of existing knowledge generates explosive streams of new conjectures. Zollman’s classic result—that sparse epistemic networks can outperform dense ones by preserving exploratory diversity—hints at a phase transition driven by the relative speeds of consensus and innovation [4].

This paper synthesises these forces into a single mathematical theory. We consider n agents holding real‑valued beliefs about a true state \theta. At each discrete time step, a new Erdős–Rényi communication graph G(n,p) is drawn independently, and beliefs are updated via a degree‑weighted DeGroot rule. Simultaneously, a pool of “live conjectures” evolves as a combinatorial birth–death process whose birth rate depends on the number of distinct ideas that can be combined within the connected components of the subgraph induced by exploring agents—those whose beliefs deviate significantly from the current population mean. A key feedback loop is introduced: each newborn idea provides a noisy signal about \theta that perturbs the beliefs of nearby agents, thus sustaining exploration.

The crucial control parameter is the effective connectivity

\rho = \frac{p}{\gamma},

where \gamma = 1 - \mathbb{E}|\lambda_2(W)| is the spectral gap of the expected influence matrix. Heuristically, p governs how easily ideas percolate, while \gamma governs how quickly beliefs homogenise. As n \to \infty we identify two critical values 0 < \rho_c^- < \rho_c^+ < \infty (depending on n and the innovation parameters) that separate three regimes:

· Stagnation (\rho < \rho_c^-): No giant connected component of exploring agents forms; the idea pool remains bounded and the network never discovers \theta.
· Discovery (\rho_c^- < \rho < \rho_c^+): A giant component percolates, the birth–death process becomes explosive, yet consensus is delayed long enough for the combinatorial explosion to generate sufficient corrective evidence. The network converges to the truth \theta in finite expected time.
· False lock‑in (\rho > \rho_c^+): Consensus forms before the idea pool can produce counter‑evidence; the network freezes at a wrong belief \hat{\theta} \neq \theta—the Zollman failure mode.

Our main technical contributions are (i) a rigorous derivation of the lower threshold \rho_c^- = 1/n using temporal percolation, (ii) a corrected explosion criterion for the birth–death idea process with linear death, and (iii) a duality relation connecting the decay of belief entropy to the Malthusian growth rate of the idea pool at the upper threshold. The precise scaling of the upper threshold remains an open problem; we present a conjecture based on the connectivity transition of the cumulative exploration graph and outline a first‑passage approach for a rigorous proof.

The rest of the paper is organised as follows. Section 2 introduces the full coupled model, with explicit feedback from ideas to beliefs and a careful treatment of the idea pool. Section 3 recalls necessary background on percolation, consensus, and birth–death processes. Section 4 states the main theorem and the entropy–explosion duality. Section 5 outlines the proof strategy. Section 6 discusses open problems and empirical implications.

