2. The Coupled Model

2.1 Agents, beliefs, and communication

Let V = \{1, \dots, n\}. Time is discrete, t = 0, 1, 2, \dots. At each step a communication graph G(t) = (V, E(t)) is drawn independently from the Erdős–Rényi ensemble \mathcal{G}(n, p). Let \deg_i(t) be the degree of agent i in G(t). The row‑stochastic influence matrix W(t) = (w_{ij}(t)) is defined by

w_{ij}(t) = \frac{1}{\deg_i(t)} \quad\text{if } (i,j) \in E(t),\qquad w_{ii}(t) = 1 \text{ if } \deg_i(t) = 0,

and 0 otherwise.

Each agent i holds a belief b_i(t) \in \mathbb{R}. The baseline belief update follows the DeGroot rule:

b(t+1) = W(t)\, b(t). \tag{1}

The true state is a fixed parameter \theta \in \mathbb{R}. Initial beliefs b_i(0) are independent random variables with mean \theta and finite variance \sigma_0^2. The population mean at time t is \bar b(t) = \frac{1}{n} \sum_i b_i(t).

2.2 Exploration set and evidence from ideas

An agent is exploring if its belief deviates significantly from the current population mean. Formally, for a fixed small threshold \varepsilon > 0,

A(t) = \{ i \in V : |b_i(t) - \bar b(t)| > \varepsilon \}.

Agents in A(t) are considered to be actively questioning the current consensus.

A pool of live conjectures (ideas) X(t) \in \mathbb{N} exists. These ideas are not permanently attached to specific agents; instead, at each moment they are instantaneously assigned uniformly at random to the agents in A(t) (the “mean‑field” assumption). This assignment is independent of the graph edges and is renewed whenever the composition of A(t) changes.

Whenever a new idea is generated, it immediately reveals a noisy signal y = \theta + \xi, with \xi \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2) independently. This signal is observed by all agents that belong to the same connected component of the subgraph G_A(t) (the subgraph of G(t) induced by A(t)) as the agent who “hosted” the idea. Those agents then update their beliefs by a convex combination of their current belief and the signal:

b_i \leftarrow (1-\eta) b_i + \eta y, \qquad \text{for each } i \text{ in that component}, \tag{2}

where \eta \in (0,1) is a learning rate. This perturbation keeps beliefs away from the consensus mean, thus sustaining exploration. If no new idea is generated during a time step, no signal is injected beyond the DeGroot update.

2.3 Idea birth–death process

Between discrete time steps t and t+1, the number of live ideas X(s), s \in [t, t+1), evolves as a continuous‑time Markov chain whose rates depend on the current exploration subgraph. The process is active only as long as A(t) is non‑empty. Let G_A(t) be the subgraph of G(t) induced by A(t).

A new idea is born when a subset of k \ge 2 distinct existing ideas happen to reside inside the same connected component of G_A(t). The total birth rate is

\lambda\bigl(X; G_A\bigr) = P \cdot \bigl( \#\{\text{$k$-subsets of ideas all in the same component}\} \bigr)^\alpha, \tag{3}

where \alpha > 0 accounts for synergy or interference, and P > 0 is a baseline productivity parameter. Each idea dies independently at rate \delta > 0; hence the total death rate is \mu(X) = \delta X.

To obtain a tractable closed form we approximate the birth rate by assuming that, for large n, only the giant component of G_A(t) (if it exists) contributes significantly. Let S(t) be the fraction of nodes of A(t) belonging to the unique giant component; if no giant component exists we set S(t) = 0. Under the mean‑field assignment, the expected number of ideas located in the giant component is X_{\text{giant}} \approx S(t) X. The number of k-subsets entirely inside the giant component is then \binom{X_{\text{giant}}}{k}. Using Stirling’s approximation for large X,

\binom{X_{\text{giant}}}{k} \approx \frac{S(t)^k X^k}{k!}.

Inserting this into (3) yields the effective birth rate

\lambda_{\rm eff}(X, t) \approx P \, \left[ S(t)^k \,\frac{X^k}{k!} \right]^{\alpha}
= \frac{P}{(k!)^{\alpha}} \, S(t)^{k\alpha} \, X^{\alpha k}. \tag{4}

We stress that this expression is valid only when a giant component exists (S(t)>0); otherwise the birth rate is determined by small components and remains O(X^{\alpha}), insufficient to trigger explosive growth for large n.

Between t and t+1, X(s) evolves with transition rates

X \to X+1 \quad\text{at rate}\quad \lambda_{\rm eff}(X, t) \cdot \mathbf{1}_{\{A(t) \neq \varnothing\}},\qquad
X \to X-1 \quad\text{at rate}\quad \delta X.

The new value X(t+1) is the state of this chain at the end of the interval. If A(t) = \varnothing the birth rate is set to zero and X cannot increase.

Initial condition. The process starts with exactly k distinct ideas, so that X(0)=k and the first combination is possible immediately. (Other small initial values yield qualitatively identical results.)

2.4 Effective connectivity and spectral gap

The speed of consensus in the pure DeGroot dynamics (without idea injections) is controlled by the spectral gap of the expected influence matrix. For the random graph ensemble, the expected matrix \bar W = \mathbb{E}[W(t)] is symmetric and doubly stochastic, with a stationary distribution \pi = (1/n,\dots,1/n). Let \lambda_2 be the second largest eigenvalue in modulus. We define the spectral gap

\gamma = 1 - |\lambda_2(\bar W)|.

For large n and p above the connectivity threshold, it is known that \gamma scales roughly as 1 - O(1/\sqrt{np}); for p = \Omega(\log n / n) we have \gamma \to 1. The mixing time—the number of steps needed for the belief vector to approach a consensus—is \tau_{\rm mix} \sim 1/\gamma.

Because the graph distribution is i.i.d., the per‑step contraction factor of the deviation from consensus is tightly concentrated around 1-\gamma. We therefore work with the constant approximation \gamma and define the dimensionless effective connectivity

\rho = \frac{p}{\gamma}. \tag{5}

All thresholds will be expressed in terms of \rho.

2.5 Summary of the dynamical system

The full state at time t is (b(t), X(t), G(t)). The evolution proceeds as follows:

1. Graph draw: G(t) \sim \mathcal{G}(n, p), independent of the past.
2. DeGroot update: b(t+\tfrac12) = W(t)\, b(t).
3. Exploration set: A(t+\tfrac12) = \{ i : |b_i(t+\tfrac12) - \bar b(t+\tfrac12)| > \varepsilon \}.
4. Idea process: The continuous‑time chain with rates \lambda_{\rm eff}(X, t)\,\mathbf{1}_{\{A \neq \varnothing\}} and \delta X runs for one unit of time. Each birth event generates a signal y = \theta + \xi; the signal is observed by all agents in the same component of G_{A}(t) as the hosting agent, and their beliefs are updated via (2).
5. Belief finalisation: b(t+1) is the belief vector after all signal injections.
6. Idea pool update: X(t+1) is the final state of the chain.

All random ingredients are conditionally independent given the previous state.

