3. Mathematical Preliminaries

3.1 Percolation thresholds

For a static Erdős–Rényi graph G(n,p), a giant component of size \Theta(n) emerges with high probability iff np > 1. The relative size S of the giant component satisfies 1 - S = \exp(-np S).

In temporal networks where edges appear independently at each step, the cumulative graph over a time window \tau possesses a giant component iff p n \tau > 1 (see Starnini et al. [2]). This result holds under the mild condition that the window length \tau is small compared to n. The event‑graph formalism maps temporal percolation to directed percolation, and the threshold retains the same scaling.

3.2 DeGroot consensus and spectral gap

If the influence matrix W is primitive, then W^t \to \mathbf{1}\pi^{\mathsf T}, where \pi is the stationary distribution. The convergence speed is governed by the spectral gap \gamma = 1 - |\lambda_2|. For degree‑weighted averaging on an Erdős–Rényi graph, Golub and Jackson [1] showed that the consensus belief equals \sum_j \pi_j b_j(0), and the network learns the truth iff \max_i \pi_i \to 0. The mixing time satisfies \tau_{\rm mix} \approx 1/\gamma.

3.3 Explosion criterion for birth–death processes

Consider a continuous‑time birth–death process with rates \lambda_n (birth) and \mu_n (death), n \ge 0. The process explodes (reaches an infinite state in finite time) almost surely if and only if

\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{\lambda_n} + \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{\mu_n}{\lambda_n \lambda_{n-1}} + \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{\mu_n \mu_{n-1}}{\lambda_n \lambda_{n-1} \lambda_{n-2}} + \cdots < \infty

(Anderson, Continuous‑Time Markov Chains, p. 263). For our model, when X is large and a giant component exists, \lambda_n \sim C n^{\alpha k} with \alpha(k-1) > 1 and \mu_n = \delta n. One verifies that all terms in the series are summable; in particular the pure‑birth series \sum 1/\lambda_n converges because \alpha k > 1, and the additional terms involving death rates decay as n^{-(\alpha k - 1)}, which is also summable. Hence the idea process explodes with probability one whenever a giant component persists. We provide a short proof in Appendix A.

3.4 The Zollman effect

Zollman [4] showed that in a network of Bayesian agents testing two actions, sparse communication structures can yield a higher probability of converging to the objectively better action than a complete graph. The reason is that dense networks share early unlucky results too rapidly, causing all agents to abandon the superior action before enough independent evidence accumulates. In our terminology, the exploration set collapses before the idea pool has time to generate corrective evidence.

