6. Discussion

The PDT framework provides a unified mathematical scaffold for understanding collective discovery in hybrid human–AI scientific networks. Several extensions are natural:

Non‑linear consensus. Real LLM agents update beliefs via attention‑weighted averaging, which resembles degree‑weighted DeGroot or bounded‑confidence models. Under irreducibility conditions, a time‑dependent spectral gap can still be defined, and our analysis carries over with the replacement of \gamma by an effective gap.

Empirical tests. The EinsteinArena public leaderboard, where human–AI teams explore conjectures, offers a test bed for estimating p, \gamma, and idea growth rates. Preliminary data suggest operation near the critical window.

Beyond mean field. The decoupling assumption that ideas are uniformly distributed among exploring agents is a simplification. In reality, ideas may cluster in subcomponents, creating a multi‑type branching process. We expect the same qualitative phase diagram, with modified constants.

The most intriguing open question is whether the entropy–explosion duality extends to a full large‑deviation principle, i.e., whether the rate function of the belief distribution and the generating function of the idea process are Legendre transforms of one another. If true, the phase diagram would be a direct manifestation of thermodynamic duality, opening the door to a statistical mechanics of epistemic networks.

