Appendix A. Notation and Model Summary

General conventions.
\mathbb{N} = \{0,1,2,\dots\}; \mathbb{R}_+ = [0,\infty); all limits are n \to \infty unless stated otherwise.
For a graph G on V = \{1,\dots,n\}, \mathcal{C}_{\max}(G) denotes its largest connected component, |\mathcal{C}_{\max}| its size. We write S(G) = |\mathcal{C}_{\max}|/|V|.
The symbol \mathbf{1}_{\{\cdot\}} is the indicator function; \mathcal{N}(\mu,\sigma^2) is the Gaussian distribution with mean \mu and variance \sigma^2.

Parameters of the model.
n – number of agents.
p \in (0,1) – edge probability of the i.i.d. Erdős–Rényi graphs G(t).
k \ge 2 – minimum number of distinct ideas required to generate a new idea.
\alpha > 0 – synergy exponent; we assume \alpha(k-1) > 1.
P > 0 – baseline productivity.
\delta \ge 0 – per‑idea death rate.
\eta \in (0,1) – learning rate for signal updates.
\sigma^2 > 0 – variance of the Gaussian signal noise.
\varepsilon > 0 – exploration threshold.

State variables.
b(t) = (b_1(t),\dots,b_n(t))^\mathsf{T} \in \mathbb{R}^n – beliefs at integer times.
X(t) \in \mathbb{N} – number of live ideas at time t.
G(t) – communication graph at step t.
A(t) = \{i : |b_i(t) - \bar b(t)| > \varepsilon\}, \bar b(t) = \frac1n\sum_i b_i(t).
a(t) = |A(t)|/n – fraction of exploring agents.

Derived quantities.
W(t) – row‑stochastic influence matrix (degree‑weighted averaging).
\bar W = \mathbb{E}[W(t)] – expected influence matrix.
\gamma = 1 - |\lambda_2(\bar W)| – spectral gap of \bar W.
\rho = p/\gamma – effective connectivity.
S(t) = S(G_{A}(t)), the relative size of the giant component of the exploration subgraph (set to 0 if none exists).

Idea dynamics.
Given G_A(t), the birth rate is defined as

\lambda(X;G_A) = P \left( \sum_{\mathcal{C} \in \text{comps}(G_A)} \binom{X_{\mathcal{C}}}{k} \right)^{\!\alpha},

where X_{\mathcal{C}} is the number of ideas that happen to lie in component \mathcal{C} under a uniform random assignment, independently of the graph edges. The effective approximation (eq. (4) in the main text) replaces the sum by the contribution of the giant component:

\lambda_{\text{eff}}(X,t) \approx P \left[ S(t)^k \frac{X^k}{k!} \right]^{\alpha}.

The continuous‑time chain X^{(t)}(s), s\in[t,t+1), runs with rates \lambda_{\text{eff}}(X,s)\,\mathbf{1}_{\{A(t)\neq\varnothing\}} (birth) and \delta X (death). Its final state is X(t+1).

