Appendix B. Formal Assumptions

1. Independence of graphs. \{G(t)\}_{t=0}^{\infty} are independent and identically distributed \mathcal{G}(n,p).
2. Initial beliefs. b_i(0) \stackrel{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} \mu_0, where \mu_0 is a probability measure on \mathbb{R} with mean \theta, finite variance \sigma_0^2, and a density with respect to Lebesgue measure. Moreover, there exists \delta_0>0 such that \mathbb{P}(|b_i(0)-\theta|>\delta_0) \ge c>0 uniformly in n.
3. Spectral gap. There exist constants \gamma_{\min},\gamma_{\max} \in (0,1] such that for all large n the spectral gap \gamma = 1 - |\lambda_2(\bar W)| satisfies \gamma_{\min} \le \gamma \le \gamma_{\max}. (For \mathcal{G}(n,p) with p = \omega(1/n), we have \gamma \to 1; the lower bound can be taken as any positive constant.)
4. Idea assignment (Mean‑Field). At every time t when A(t)\neq\varnothing, the X(t) ideas are assigned independently and uniformly to the agents in A(t). This assignment is redrawn at the beginning of each continuous‑time interval and is independent of G(t).
5. Combinatorial explosion condition. \alpha(k-1) > 1, ensuring that the pure‑birth component of the idea process is explosive when a giant component is present.

