Appendix C. Proof of the Lower Threshold

C.1. Mean‑Field Decoupling Lemma

Lemma 1. Condition on the history up to time t and on the event \{A(t) = A\} for a deterministic set A \subseteq V of size a n. The subgraph G_A(t) (the induced subgraph on A) has the same distribution as an Erdős–Rényi graph \mathcal{G}(|A|, p), independently of the precise identities of the agents in A.

Proof. Since G(t) is drawn unconditionally from \mathcal{G}(n,p), every pair of vertices is connected independently with probability p. Restricting to vertices in A retains this independence; the edges among them are a random graph on |A| vertices with edge probability p, and this graph is independent of the set A because edge indicators are independent of vertex labels. ∎

Thus, conditional on a(t), the exploration subgraph is a \mathcal{G}(a n, p) random graph. Percolation properties (existence and size of the giant component) can be studied through the usual Erdős–Rényi phase transition, with effective parameter p \cdot a n.

C.2. Temporal Percolation Threshold

Consider a single exploring agent’s trajectory. While exploring, its belief evolves under the DeGroot dynamics, which contracts deviations towards the mean. In the absence of signal injections, the maximum possible lifetime of an agent in A(t) (i.e., the time until its deviation falls below \varepsilon) is of order \tau \sim 1/\gamma (see Lemma 3 below). Therefore, a natural time window for forming a giant component among explorers is \tau \approx c/\gamma for some constant c>0.

The cumulative graph over \tau steps, \mathcal{G}_{\text{cum}} = \bigcup_{s=t}^{t+\tau-1} G_A(s), has edge probability

p_{\text{cum}} = 1 - (1-p)^\tau \ge \frac{p\tau}{2}

for p\tau small enough, which is the regime of interest. If p\tau \cdot a n > 1, the cumulative graph contains a giant component with high probability (Starnini et al., [2]). Substituting \tau \sim 1/\gamma and initially a \approx 1, this condition becomes

\frac{p}{\gamma} n > 1 \quad \Longleftrightarrow \quad \rho n > 1.

Hence we define

\rho_c^- = \frac{1}{n}.

If \rho < \rho_c^-, no giant component can form over the lifespan of any cohort of explorers; the idea birth rate remains bounded by O(X^\alpha) (coming from small components) and the process cannot explode. The formal proof is completed by coupling the idea process to a subcritical branching process and applying Markov’s inequality; we omit the standard details.

C.3. Stagnation Regime

Proposition 1. Assume \rho \le (1-\varepsilon)/n for some \varepsilon>0. Then with probability 1-o(1),

1. \sup_t X(t) < \infty a.s.
2. There exists a random time T<\infty such that A(t)=\varnothing for all t\ge T.
3. \lim_{t\to\infty} \bar b(t) \neq \theta.

Proof sketch. The condition implies that for any fixed t, the effective parameter p a n \le p n < 1-\varepsilon, so the exploration subgraph is subcritical. Standard percolation results give that the largest component has size O(\log n). Therefore, even if all ideas are placed in one component, the birth rate is at most P \cdot \binom{O(\log n)}{k}^\alpha = O((\log n)^{\alpha k}), which is negligible compared to the linear death rate \delta X when X is large. Hence the idea pool cannot grow; it behaves like a subcritical birth–death process, so \limsup X(t) < \infty.

Once ideas stop being produced, no further signal injections occur. The pure DeGroot dynamics then drive all beliefs to a consensus exponentially fast, emptying A(t). The consensus value is a weighted average of initial beliefs and any early signals. By the law of large numbers, the initial average is close to \theta, but the absence of sufficient corrective signals (due to bounded idea pool) means that with positive probability the consensus is not exactly \theta; the system freezes at some \hat\theta \neq \theta. ∎

