Appendix D. Explosion Criterion for the Idea Process

We study the continuous‑time birth–death process Y(t) with state space \mathbb{N} and rates

q_{n,n+1} = \lambda_n, \qquad q_{n,n-1} = \mu_n = \delta n,

where \lambda_n = c n^{\beta} for large n, with \beta>1 and c>0. We claim that this process explodes almost surely.

Theorem D.1 (Feller–Lundberg criterion). A birth–death process with rates \lambda_n,\mu_n (where \lambda_n>0 for all n) explodes with probability 1 if and only if

\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{\lambda_n} + \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{\mu_n}{\lambda_n \lambda_{n-1}} + \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{\mu_n \mu_{n-1}}{\lambda_n \lambda_{n-1} \lambda_{n-2}} + \cdots < \infty.
\tag{D.1}

Proof. See Anderson, Continuous‑Time Markov Chains, Chapter 2, Theorem 2.2. ∎

Apply this to our model with \beta = \alpha k > 1. We check each series:

1. S_1 = \sum \frac{1}{\lambda_n} \sim \sum n^{-\beta} converges.
2. S_2 = \sum \frac{\mu_n}{\lambda_n \lambda_{n-1}} \sim \frac{\delta}{c^2} \sum n^{1-2\beta}. Since 2\beta-1 > 1 (as \beta>1), this converges.
3. For a general term of order m:

\frac{\mu_n \mu_{n-1} \cdots \mu_{n-m+1}}{\lambda_n \lambda_{n-1} \cdots \lambda_{n-m}} \sim \frac{\delta^m}{c^{m+1}} \, n^{m - (m+1)\beta}.

The exponent is -\beta - m(\beta-1). Since \beta>1, all terms are summable over n. Hence the entire infinite sum is finite, and explosion occurs a.s.

For our effective birth rate \lambda_{\text{eff}}(X) \sim \frac{P}{(k!)^{\alpha}} S^{k\alpha} X^{\alpha k} with S>0, the same conclusion holds because S^{k\alpha}>0 is a constant. Thus, whenever the giant component persists (so that S is bounded away from zero), the idea process reaches infinity in finite time with probability 1.

