Appendix E. Upper Threshold and Discovery Regime

E.1. Deterministic ODE Limit

We derive the mean‑field ODEs (8) and (9) via a standard fluid limit argument, assuming n\to\infty.

Define the empirical measures of beliefs and ideas. Because the number of agents is large, we approximate the exploration fraction a(t) by a deterministic function \mathbf{a}(t). Conditional on \mathbf{a}(t) > 0, the giant component size S(t) is the solution of

1 - S = \exp\bigl(-\rho \gamma n \, \mathbf{a}(t) \, S\bigr).

Now, the per‑step contraction of deviations from consensus in the DeGroot model gives (see Lemma 3 below)

\Delta \mathbf{a}_{\text{cons}} \le -c_1 \gamma \mathbf{a},

for some constant c_1>0. The signal injection mechanism: each birth event delivers a noisy signal to a fraction \approx S of explorers, resetting their deviations to values of order \eta |\xi|. A detailed calculation shows that an agent receiving a signal re‑enters A(t) with probability at least q>0 (depending on \eta,\sigma,\varepsilon), provided its current deviation was small. Hence the positive contribution to \mathbf{a} per unit time is at least

\text{birth rate} \times S \times \frac{q}{n}.

Using the effective birth rate \lambda_{\text{eff}}, we obtain

\frac{d\mathbf{a}}{dt} = -c_1 \gamma \mathbf{a} + \frac{q}{n} \cdot \frac{P}{(k!)^{\alpha}} S^{k\alpha} X^{\alpha k} \cdot S \cdot \mathbf{1}_{\{\mathbf{a}>0\}}.

Absorbing constants yields eq. (8) with C_1 = q P/(k!)^{\alpha}.

The idea pool evolves according to

\frac{dX}{dt} \approx \frac{P}{(k!)^{\alpha}} S^{k\alpha} X^{\alpha k} - \delta X,

justifying eq. (9). These ODEs provide a qualitative picture of the dynamics.

E.2. Time‑Scale Balance and the Upper Threshold

The discovery window exists only if the timescale for the first innovation \tau_{\text{birth}} is shorter than the consensus‑induced extinction time of the exploration set \tau_{\text{cons}}.

Consensus time. From Lemma 3, once the idea pool is quiescent, a(t) decays exponentially at rate \gamma. Starting from a(0)\approx 1, it drops below the percolation threshold a_c = 1/(\rho \gamma n) after time

\tau_{\text{cons}} \approx \frac{1}{\gamma} \log\bigl( \rho \gamma n \bigr).

(Here we used a_c = 1/(p n) = 1/(\rho \gamma n).)

Innovation time. At the beginning, X(0)=k, and the birth rate is \lambda_0 = \frac{P}{(k!)^{\alpha}} S_0^{k\alpha}, where S_0 is the giant component fraction at full exploration (a=1). The time to the first birth is exponential with mean 1/\lambda_0. Thus, typically \tau_{\text{birth}} \approx \lambda_0^{-1}.

Discovery requires \tau_{\text{birth}} \ll \tau_{\text{cons}}, i.e.

\frac{(k!)^{\alpha}}{P\, S_0^{k\alpha}} \ll \frac{1}{\gamma} \log(\rho \gamma n). \tag{E.1}

The factor S_0 depends on \rho \gamma n through the ER giant component equation. For \rho \gamma n just above 1, S_0 is small, S_0 \approx 2(\rho \gamma n - 1) near the threshold. As \rho increases, S_0 grows and eventually saturates near 1. The left‑hand side of (E.1) decreases with \rho, while the right‑hand side increases. Hence the inequality eventually holds for sufficiently large \rho, provided the intrinsic productivity P is not too small relative to \gamma. However, if \gamma is also increasing (since \gamma \to 1 as p grows), the right‑hand side shrinks. A detailed asymptotic analysis near the connectivity transition (expanding S_0 and \gamma in terms of p n) shows that the inequality is satisfied iff

p n \gtrsim C \log n,

for a constant C that depends on P,\delta,k,\alpha. This is exactly the connectivity threshold of the static exploration graph, leading to the conjecture

\rho_c^+(n) = \frac{C \log n}{n}(1+o(1)).

A rigorous proof that a sharp phase transition occurs at this scaling requires a more refined large‑deviation estimate for the branching process, which is work in progress.

E.3. Large Deviations and False Lock‑In

For \rho > \rho_c^+ (i.e., p n \gg \log n), the exploration graph is dense and the consensus time \tau_{\text{cons}} is very short. The first idea birth, however, still requires an exponential time of order 1/\lambda_0. There is a non‑negligible probability that the random exponential clock for the first birth exceeds \tau_{\text{cons}}; then the exploration set collapses before any corrective signal is generated. This event leads to permanent false lock‑in.

To bound this probability from below, we use a quasi‑decoupling argument. Because the graph at each step is independent of the idea positions conditionally on A(t), we can condition on the worst‑case scenario for consensus: the graph sequence causes unusually fast contraction. Let \mathcal{E} be the event that the cumulative number of edges among explorers over the time interval [0,\tau_{\text{cons}}] is so small that the DeGroot process mixes faster than typical. This event has probability \ge e^{-c \tau_{\text{cons}}} for some c>0. On \mathcal{E}, the exploration fraction a(t) drops below the percolation threshold before any birth can occur. Since the birth clock is independent of \mathcal{E} conditionally on the initial state, the probability of lock‑in is at least

\mathbb{P}(\text{no birth by }\tau_{\text{cons}} \mid \mathcal{E}) \, \mathbb{P}(\mathcal{E}) \ge e^{-\lambda_0 \tau_{\text{cons}}} \, e^{-c\tau_{\text{cons}}}.

As n\to\infty, \tau_{\text{cons}} \sim (1/\gamma)\log n remains finite, so this lower bound stays positive. Hence false lock‑in occurs with probability bounded away from zero.

