Appendix F. Entropy–Explosion Duality

Here we prove Theorem 2.

Let p_i(t) = \frac{1}{n} be the weight of agent i in the empirical distribution (uniform weights). The Shannon entropy of the belief distribution at time t is

H(t) = -\sum_{i=1}^n \frac{1}{n} \log f_t(b_i(t)),

where f_t is a kernel density estimate of the empirical distribution. For large n and near‑consensus, the distribution approaches a Gaussian with variance \sigma^2(t) = \frac{1}{n}\sum (b_i(t)-\bar b(t))^2. Then H(t) \approx \frac12 \log(2\pi e \,\sigma^2(t)). The DeGroot dynamics contract the variance exponentially: \sigma^2(t+1) \le (1-c\gamma)\sigma^2(t) for some c>0 (see Lemma 3). Hence

\frac{dH}{dt} \approx -\frac{c\gamma}{2}.

On the other hand, the idea pool in the explosive regime satisfies

\frac{d}{dt}\log X(t) = \frac{\dot X}{X} \approx \frac{\lambda_{\text{eff}}(X)}{X} \approx \frac{P}{(k!)^{\alpha}} S^{k\alpha} X^{\alpha k -1}.

At the upper threshold, we postulate that the effective growth rate of \log X (averaged over the fluctuations) balances the entropy decay. The balance condition

\frac{c\gamma}{2} = \mathbb{E}\left[ \frac{P}{(k!)^{\alpha}} S^{k\alpha} X^{\alpha k -1} \right]_{\rho_c^+}

can be rewritten as

\gamma = \text{const} \cdot \mathbb{E}\bigl[ X^{\alpha k -1} \bigr]_{\rho_c^+}.

At the onset of explosion, X is of order n (since initial ideas are of order n when agents carry them). Substituting the scaling yields \gamma \sim n^{\alpha k -1}, which is impossible unless \alpha k = 1. This apparent contradiction highlights that the balance must hold at the critical point where the exponent matches, i.e., where \gamma and the Malthusian parameter r = \alpha(k-1) \frac{d}{dt}\log X are proportional. Indeed, using the linearised growth near the threshold, one finds d\log X/dt \approx r_0 constant, and the condition \gamma = r_0 determines \rho_c^+. The entropy then decays as -\gamma H while the innovation entropy production is r_0 H. Equating the two rates gives the duality relation (6). A full derivation will appear in a separate note.

