Appendix G. Auxiliary Lemmas

Lemma 2 (Spectral gap for ER graphs). For \mathcal{G}(n,p) with p = \omega(\log n/n), the expected degree‑weighted influence matrix \bar W satisfies

|\lambda_2(\bar W)| \le \frac{C}{\sqrt{np}} + o(1),

where C is a universal constant. Consequently, \gamma = 1 - |\lambda_2| is bounded away from 0 and approaches 1 as np\to\infty.

Proof. This follows from the eigenvalue analysis of the normalised Laplacian of Erdős–Rényi graphs; see Chung & Lu, Complex Graphs and Networks, Theorem 5.4. ∎

Lemma 3 (Contraction of deviations in DeGroot). Under the degree‑weighted averaging rule (1), for any initial belief vector b,

\sum_{i=1}^n |b_i(t+1) - \bar b(t+1)|^2 \le (1 - \gamma) \sum_{i=1}^n |b_i(t) - \bar b(t)|^2,

where \gamma is the spectral gap of \mathbb{E}[W(t)], and the inequality holds in expectation over the graph randomness, and with high probability conditional on the past.

Proof. Standard. The expected squared deviation from consensus contracts by at least the spectral gap of the expected matrix; see Golub & Jackson [1], Lemma 4. The almost‑sure concentration follows from the i.i.d. nature of the edges and the boundedness of beliefs (which can be enforced by truncation). ∎

Lemma 4 (Perturbation by a signal). Suppose an agent with belief b receives a noisy signal y = \theta + \xi with \xi\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^2) and updates via b' = (1-\eta)b + \eta y. If |b - \bar b| \le \varepsilon/2 and |\bar b - \theta| \le \varepsilon/4, then |b' - \bar b'| > \varepsilon with probability at least q(\eta,\sigma,\varepsilon) > 0, provided \bar b' is the updated population mean. Consequently, signal injection repopulates the exploration set.

Proof. The deviation after update is approximately \eta|y-\bar b| plus a small drift from the mean shift. Since y = \theta + \xi and \theta differs from \bar b by at most \varepsilon/4, we have |\eta\xi| large enough with positive probability. ∎

